          {
            ":db/id": 17592186069503,
            ":step/content": {
              ":db/id": 17592186069887,
              ":tutorial/content": "Now consider the function $y=h(x)$ below. This function has a discontinuity at $x=0$, so it is not continuous over its entire domain.\n\n![](image_assets/3ea09156af8b143260f3c32e921edb31-4e41ca41e4.png)\n\nHowever, the function $h(x)$ may be continuous at other points, such as $x=1$.\n\nTo determine whether $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$, we have to consider points in the **neighborhood** of $x=1$. Roughly speaking, the *neighborhood of $x=1$* means the points on the curve that have an $x$-coordinate that's close to $1$.\n\nIndeed, in the *neighborhood* of $x=1$, we can draw the function $h(x)$ without taking the pencil off the paper. So, $h(x)$ is indeed continuous at $x=1$.",
              ":tutorial/id": "54ca4bef-1b8e-5315-85d4-0667910b332c",
              ":tutorial/math-academy-id": 2640,
              ":tutorial/title": "Continuity at a Point"
